From dedf14d74d2825a92e66803d5e020f369638ea7f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 14:33:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Checker: give the scratch back after the reader past what
 the next stages push again (#628)

After the reader the scratch holds the parse's and the reader's pages,
dead, which check-expressions, check-declarations and static-data
pushed too little to reuse, and held to static-data's end. The driver
now gives them back after the reader (check_driver_discard_scratch),
past 96 bytes a token beyond the scratch's length: those stages and
check_program push about 64 MB of sqlite3.c's again before
check_program gives the scratch back, which they would otherwise fault
in again from cleared huge pages (15 M kernel cycles on the 9700X for a
whole discard; buster-performance's scratch-rare-628-2026-11-07). A
lane of several units with another to compile gives them back in whole
huge pages, as check_program does (#777), asking check_units_unit_left,
which check_units_discard_huge's count of check_program's discards
doesn't count. The common warnings pop what they push, which the
backend's pushes then reuse rather than push past.

On the VM, static-data's end falls from 256.6 to 233.5 MB.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01NNmNQwDnVuEpjsYvVE2CKz
Signed-off-by: David Gonzalez Martin <davidgmbb@gmail.com>
